# ATLAS Docker Compose Configuration  —  DOCKER COMPOSE DEPLOYMENT
#
# This file is read primarily by `docker compose`; the atlas CLI also
# reads it for port/model resolution (so bare `atlas` commands work on
# non-default ports). It is NOT read by the K3s installer
# (./scripts/install.sh) — that path uses atlas.conf. Values placed in
# atlas.conf are silently ignored by the docker path. See ISSUES.md PC-021.
#
# Preferred: run `atlas init`, which copies this file and fills the selected
# model plus hardware-aware sizing. For manual setup, copy it and fill every
# field marked required before starting Compose.

# Path to GGUF model weights on host. `atlas init` fills the model fields
# from the registry selection; manual installs must set both explicitly.
ATLAS_MODELS_DIR=./models

# Model filename (required; must exist in ATLAS_MODELS_DIR)
ATLAS_MODEL_FILE=

# Model identifier (required; normally the filename without .gguf)
ATLAS_MODEL_NAME=

# Context window size (tokens) — TOTAL across all parallel slots.
# llama-server divides this by ATLAS_PARALLEL_SLOTS for per-slot context.
# Size it (and the runtime-sizing keys below) for YOUR model + GPU with:
#   atlas tier fit --write
# The server runs with --fit off: an oversized budget refuses to start
# instead of silently spilling layers to CPU (5x slower generation).
ATLAS_CTX_SIZE=131072

# macOS Metal only: native llama.cpp install prefix. The setup script,
# launcher, and doctor all resolve this value. Override when setup used
# `--prefix`; ignored by container inference backends.
# ATLAS_MACOS_PREFIX=~/.atlas/macos

# --- Runtime sizing (PC-208) — written by `atlas tier fit --write` ---
# Concurrent request slots (per-slot ctx = ATLAS_CTX_SIZE / slots)
# ATLAS_PARALLEL_SLOTS=4
# KV-cache quantization: f16 | q8_0 | q4_0
# ATLAS_KV_TYPE_K=f16
# ATLAS_KV_TYPE_V=f16
# Micro-batch (-ub): drives the compute-buffer VRAM cost
# ATLAS_UBATCH=1024
# Logical batch (-b)
# Must be <= ATLAS_UBATCH because self-embeddings are always enabled.
# ATLAS_BATCH=1024

# Structural call-graph reasoning (#39, EXPERIMENTAL, default off). When 1/true,
# v3-service builds a precomputed call graph and uses it to deepen the structural
# veto, the repair context, and symbol-index injection. Strictly additive — off
# leaves behavior unchanged. See docs/reports/CALL_GRAPH_REASONING_V3.md.
# ATLAS_CALL_GRAPH=0

# V3.2 RPG-style architecture-first planning (#120, EXPERIMENTAL, default off).
# When 1/true, planning runs a two-stage Repository Planning Graph (modules ->
# files -> signatures -> data-flow edges) ahead of problem-level PlanSearch, and
# threads each node's planned interface into generation. Strictly additive — off
# leaves planning/generation unchanged. See docs/reports/RPG_WAVELET_PLANNING_V3_2.md.
# ATLAS_RPG_PLANNING=0

# Host project directory bind-mounted to /workspace inside the proxy container.
# This is the directory ATLAS reads and writes files in. The bind mount is
# resolved at `docker compose up` time and is fixed for the container's
# lifetime. To switch projects:
#   ATLAS_PROJECT_DIR=/path/to/proj docker compose up -d --force-recreate atlas-proxy
# Defaults to the directory you ran `docker compose` from.
# ATLAS_PROJECT_DIR=/path/to/your/project

# --- Image source (PC-052) ---
# Container images are pulled from GHCR by default. Override these to:
#   - pin to a versioned release (recommended for production)
#   - track the dev branch (bleeding edge)
#   - point at a fork's published images (e.g., for testing your own build)
# See docs/SETUP.md "Image source" for the full behavior matrix.

# GHCR namespace. Leave as `itigges22` for upstream images. Set to your
# own GitHub username if you've forked the repo and pushed your own
# images via the build-images.yml workflow.
# ATLAS_GHCR_OWNER=itigges22

# Image tag. Defaults to `latest` (tracks bleeding edge from main).
# Pin to a specific tag for stability:
#   ATLAS_IMAGE_TAG=3.1.3        # versioned release (registry semver tags carry no leading v)
#   ATLAS_IMAGE_TAG=sha-abc1234  # exact commit
#   ATLAS_IMAGE_TAG=dev          # bleeding edge from dev branch
# ATLAS_IMAGE_TAG=latest

# --- Runtime tuning (optional; empty/unset = in-code default) ---
# Inference backend the llama-server entrypoint tunes for:
# cuda | rocm | vulkan | metal (macOS hybrid)
# ATLAS_BACKEND=cuda
# GPU index on multi-GPU hosts (maps to CUDA/HIP/Vulkan visible-devices)
# ATLAS_GPU_INDEX=0
# Tool-call grammar mode: strict (schema GBNF, default) | loose (valid-JSON-only).
# loose is REQUIRED for Gemma-family models — strict schema-GBNF causes done-spam.
# ATLAS_GRAMMAR_MODE=strict
# Sandbox memory cap (e.g. 24g). `atlas init` writes ~75% of host RAM.
# Conservative default is 4g so a raw compose up is never unlimited.
# ATLAS_SANDBOX_MEM=4g
# Sandbox process-count cap (fork-bomb backstop)
# ATLAS_SANDBOX_PIDS=1024
# Per-call execution ceiling (seconds) inside the sandbox executor;
# default 300 matches the proxy's run_command cap
# ATLAS_SANDBOX_MAX_EXECUTION_TIME=300
# Sandbox tmpfs size ceilings (ceilings, not reservations — written
# bytes count against ATLAS_SANDBOX_MEM). PIP_SIZE is ~/.local, the
# `pip install --user` target: heavy scientific installs (pandas +
# pyarrow) need ~400 MB+.
# ATLAS_SANDBOX_TMP_SIZE=2G
# ATLAS_SANDBOX_PIP_SIZE=1G
# ATLAS_SANDBOX_CACHE_SIZE=512M
# V3 bridge call timeout in seconds (0 disables the cap)
# ATLAS_V3_TIMEOUT=
# Per-turn generation ceiling (tokens)
# ATLAS_MAX_TOKENS=
# Conversation-history token budget override for the agent loop
# ATLAS_AGENT_HISTORY_BUDGET=
# Repetition-control sampling. llama-server ships every one of these off, so
# the proxy sets DRY on outgoing requests to bound repeating generations.
# DRY rather than repeat_penalty: repeat_penalty scores individual tokens and
# punishes the indentation and keywords that source code repeats legitimately,
# while DRY scores repeated sequences and treats "\n" as a break.
# Set ATLAS_DRY_MULTIPLIER=0 to turn DRY off entirely.
# ATLAS_DRY_MULTIPLIER=0.8
# ATLAS_DRY_BASE=1.75
# Longest sequence that may repeat unpenalized (llama.cpp default 2 is too
# tight for code, where 3-token runs are ordinary)
# ATLAS_DRY_ALLOWED_LENGTH=6
# DRY lookback in tokens; bounded rather than -1, which would make every
# earlier turn in the conversation count as a repetition source
# ATLAS_DRY_PENALTY_LAST_N=2048
# Classic repetition penalty, off by default (1.0). Available for repeated
# whitespace, which DRY's newline break cannot see. Non-1.0 also sends
# ATLAS_REPEAT_LAST_N.
# ATLAS_REPEAT_PENALTY=1.0
# ATLAS_REPEAT_LAST_N=64
# Labeled-sample count at which the lens "retrain available" prompt fires
# ATLAS_LENS_RETRAIN_MIN=2000
# Set 0 to disable the proxy's 45s llama-server keep-warm ping
# ATLAS_KEEP_LLAMA_WARM=1
# Set 0 to reuse llama KV slots across sessions instead of erasing
# ATLAS_FRESH_SLOT_PER_SESSION=1
# Set 0 to disable within-session duplicate-read elision
# ATLAS_DEDUP_READS=1
# Cap agent loop turns (0 = uncapped for tool-using tiers)
# ATLAS_MAX_TURNS=0
# run_command executor: "sandbox" (default) or "host" (drops the container backstop)
# ATLAS_VERIFY_IN=sandbox
# Command-execution trust: untrusted (refuse run_command) | trusted
# (sandbox, default) | fully-trusted (allow host exec with ATLAS_VERIFY_IN=host).
# Compose forwards this to the proxy container.
# ATLAS_TRUST_MODE=trusted
# Host dir the proxy writes lens training samples into
# ATLAS_LENS_HOST_DIR=./lens_training
# Container-side dir the proxy writes lens training samples into (default /data/lens_training)
# ATLAS_LENS_DATA_DIR=/data/lens_training
# read_file per-call byte cap; default derives from the context budget, clamped to [2 KB, 200 KB]
# ATLAS_MAX_READ_BYTES=
# Path to the ASA steering-vector .gguf inside the llama container (registry pins per-model hashes)
# ATLAS_CONTROL_VECTOR=/models/ast_edit_steering.gguf
# Host dir holding the lens weight bundle (doctor checks the same dir Docker mounts)
# ATLAS_LENS_MODELS=./geometric-lens/geometric_lens/models
# Lens SQLite state store (pattern cache, co-occurrence graph, router
# posteriors, task queue, metrics). Compose pins the container path to the
# lens-state volume; this knob is for host/dev runs of the lens service.
# Default: /data/state/geometric_state.db when /data/state exists, else
# ./geometric_state.db in the working directory.
# SQLITE_DB_PATH=/data/state/geometric_state.db
# Sandbox runtime identity + CPU quota. `atlas init` writes uid/gid and
# ~75% of host cores; a raw compose up defaults to uid/gid 1000 and
# 2 CPUs (0 = unlimited — not recommended).
# ATLAS_SANDBOX_UID=1000
# ATLAS_SANDBOX_GID=1000
# ATLAS_SANDBOX_CPUS=2
# Set true to cut sandbox egress (executed code loses all network; the
# host port publish goes dead — see docs/CONFIGURATION.md)
# ATLAS_SANDBOX_NET_INTERNAL=false
# Host dir mounted read-only at /run/atlas-secrets in every service.
# Holds service-token (internal auth, generated by `atlas init`).
# The token VALUE never goes in this file.
# ATLAS_SECRETS_DIR=./secrets

# --- TUI (read by `atlas tui`, not by docker compose) ---
# Mouse capture: on (default; wheel scroll) | off (terminal-native text selection)
# ATLAS_TUI_MOUSE=on
# Path for the TUI debug log (same as --log; empty = disabled)
# ATLAS_TUI_LOG=
# atlas-proxy base URL the TUI connects to (default http://localhost:8090)
# ATLAS_PROXY_URL=http://localhost:8090

# Service ports (change if you have conflicts)
ATLAS_LLAMA_PORT=8080
# Native macOS inference binds to loopback by default. Override only when
# access from another host is intentionally required.
# ATLAS_LLAMA_HOST=127.0.0.1
ATLAS_LENS_PORT=8099
ATLAS_V3_PORT=8070
ATLAS_SANDBOX_PORT=30820
ATLAS_PROXY_PORT=8090
